<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Canton of Appenzell</h1><p class="st-meta">1403 CE – 1597 CE · capital Appenzell</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Alpine land of free peasants who threw off the rule of the abbot of Saint Gall in the Appenzell Wars and joined the Swiss in 1513.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Appenzell districts belonged to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/imperial-abbey-of-saint-gall">Imperial Abbey of Saint Gall</a>. From 1401 the peasants rebelled against the abbot's officials and, with help from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/canton-of-schwyz">Canton of Schwyz</a>, defeated his and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-habsburg">Habsburg</a> forces at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-vogelinsegg">Vögelinsegg</a> in 1403 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-stoss">Stoss</a> in 1405. Their League above the Lake briefly spread across eastern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/swiss-confederation">Switzerland</a> before its defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bregenz">Bregenz</a> in 1408.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Appenzell governed itself through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landsgemeinde">Landsgemeinde</a>, the assembly of all men, which elected the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landammann">Landammann</a>. It became an associate of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/confederate-states-of-america">Confederacy</a> in 1411 and a full member, the thirteenth canton, in 1513. The economy rested on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and dairy farming and later on linen weaving. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> split the inner, Catholic districts from the outer, Reformed ones.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>To avoid civil war the canton was divided in 1597, with Swiss mediation, into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/appenzell-innerrhoden">Appenzell Innerrhoden</a>, which stayed Catholic, and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/appenzell-ausserrhoden">Appenzell Ausserrhoden</a>, which became Reformed. The two half-cantons still exist.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/canton-of-appenzell.jpg" alt="Canton of Appenzell" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Canton_of_Appenzell_Innerrhoden.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1403 CE – 1597 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Appenzell</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, Reformed Protestantism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swiss German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Switzerland</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Appenzell</dd></dl></aside></div>
